    "**(d) is guaranteed shortest:** BFS explores all words of length $k$ before length $k+1$, so the first synchronizing word found is necessarily of minimum length.\n",
    "\n",
    "**(e) is not guaranteed shortest:** the greedy construction merges one pair at a time, where each individual merging word is shortest for that pair. However, stitching them together introduces redundancy, a suffix of one merging word may overlap with a prefix of the next, or states may already be merged as a side effect of an earlier word, making later letters unnecessary.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Heuristic: Trimming Redundant Suffix Letters\n",
    "## Algorithm \n",
    "`still_synchronizes(A, word)` acts as a subroutine throughout:\n",
    "1. Begin with the full state set $Q$\n",
    "2. Apply each letter in `word` in sequence, replacing the current set with its image under $\\tau$\n",
    "3. Return `True` if and only if the final set is a singleton\n",
    "\n",
    "`trim_word(A, word)` uses this to greedily shorten the word from (e):\n",
    "1. Make a working copy of the input word and set the scan index $i$ to the final position\n",
    "2. Build a candidate word by removing the letter at position $i$\n",
    "3. Test the candidate with `still_synchronizes`, if it still synchronizes, the letter at position $i$ was redundant and is permanently removed; the index stays at $i$ (since the list has shifted, the next unchecked letter is now at the same position)\n",
    "4. If the candidate fails, the letter at position $i$ is genuinely needed, decrement $i$ and move left\n",
    "5. Repeat until $i < 0$, then return the trimmed word\n",
    "\n",
    "Scanning **backwards** rather than forwards avoids an index correction problem: removing a letter at position $i$ shifts all letters to its right, but since those have already been processed, no re-checking is needed. Scanning forwards would require manually adjusting $i$ after every successful removal.\n",
    "\n",
    "The heuristic is **not guaranteed to find the global minimum**, whether a letter is removable depends on which earlier letters were already removed, so the result is order-dependent. A letter that is redundant in isolation may become necessary once a neighbouring letter has been removed, and vice versa."
   ]
  },
